  <data key="d173">2026-09-28</data>
  <data key="d79">When human line-by-line reading no longer scales, independently justified checks and accountable approval must replace its assurance role.</data>
  <data key="d80">Predictions and vendor/company statistics are reported second-hand; do not treat a risk-tier policy as externally validated.</data>
  <data key="d81">Use explicit risk tiers and an evidence dossier rather than assuming every line is read; Keep independent tests and a named human acceptance authority; Protect mentoring, explainability and reviewer capacity.</data>
  <data key="d82">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d86">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d85">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d83">A</data>
  <data key="d87">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d93">Osmani revises his earlier advice to inspect every generated line: routine low-risk changes may receive automated first-pass review, while high-blast-radius changes retain deeper scrutiny. He argues that review also transmitted knowledge and that engineering teams must retain both understanding and authority to refuse a release.</data>
  <data key="d264">When human line-by-line reading no longer scales, independently justified checks and accountable approval must replace its assurance role.</data>
  <data key="d265">Use explicit risk tiers and an evidence dossier rather than assuming every line is read; Keep independent tests and a named human acceptance authority; Protect mentoring, explainability and reviewer capacity.</data>
