<node id="O010">
  <data key="d73">Agentic Autonomy Levels</data>
  <data key="d74">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d96">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d75">What agents can do</data>
  <data key="d76">2026</data>
  <data key="d77">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d78">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-autonomy-levels</data>
  <data key="d168">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-autonomy-levels</data>
  <data key="d226">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-autonomy-levels</data>
  <data key="d173">2026-07-03</data>
  <data key="d79">Osmani separates autonomy into agency and orchestration axes, describing six operating levels from suggestions to management-by-exception.</data>
  <data key="d80">Practitioner interpretation; not primary experimental evidence.</data>
  <data key="d81">Grade individual-agent initiative separately from multi-agent orchestration; Use the six levels as a contextual operational taxonomy, not a validated maturity index; Set an explicit risk and verification ceiling for delegated work.</data>
  <data key="d82">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d86">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d85">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d83">A</data>
  <data key="d87">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d93">Osmani argues that a one-dimensional autonomy ladder hides the difference between what one agent may do and the ability to coordinate many agents. He proposes two axes—agency and orchestration—and six practical levels: assist, supervised action, scoped task delegation, goal-driven autonomy, parallel delegation, and managed-by-exception orchestration. Risk, reversibility, independent checks and escalation limit the appropriate level for any task.</data>
  <data key="d264">Osmani separates autonomy into agency and orchestration axes, describing six operating levels from suggestions to management-by-exception.</data>
  <data key="d265">Grade individual-agent initiative separately from multi-agent orchestration; Use the six levels as a contextual operational taxonomy, not a validated maturity index; Set an explicit risk and verification ceiling for delegated work.</data>
  <data key="d84">2026</data>
  <data key="d92">3.3</data>
  <data key="d266">C03</data>
  <data key="d267">Direct</data>
  <data key="d94">curated practitioner article (not a research study)</data>
