<node id="O012">
  <data key="d73">Loop Engineering</data>
  <data key="d74">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d96">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d75">Engineering structure</data>
  <data key="d76">2026</data>
  <data key="d77">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d78">https://addyo.substack.com/p/loop-engineering</data>
  <data key="d168">https://addyo.substack.com/p/loop-engineering</data>
  <data key="d226">https://addyo.substack.com/p/loop-engineering</data>
  <data key="d173">2026-06-08</data>
  <data key="d79">Osmani identifies five interoperable loop-building primitives—scheduling, worktrees, skills, connectors and subagents—plus durable external memory.</data>
  <data key="d80">A practitioner engineering pattern; the article does not establish comparative loop reliability by controlled experiment.</data>
  <data key="d81">Schedule discovery and triage but bound side effects; Isolate parallel work in worktrees; share intentions via tested skills/connectors and durable state; Separate the maker from the checker, measure token usage, and escalate when independent checks fail.</data>
  <data key="d82">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d86">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d85">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d83">A</data>
  <data key="d87">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d93">Loop engineering means configuring a system that discovers work, dispatches agents, checks outputs, records progress and repeats rather than manually prompting each step. Osmani identifies five recurring primitives: automations/scheduling, isolated Git worktrees, reusable skills, plugins and connectors, and specialized subagents. A separate persistent memory or task board is the sixth requirement for continuity. He also warns about verification, comprehension debt and unpredictable token costs.</data>
  <data key="d264">Osmani identifies five interoperable loop-building primitives—scheduling, worktrees, skills, connectors and subagents—plus durable external memory.</data>
  <data key="d265">Schedule discovery and triage but bound side effects; Isolate parallel work in worktrees; share intentions via tested skills/connectors and durable state; Separate the maker from the checker, measure token usage, and escalate when independent checks fail.</data>
  <data key="d84">2026</data>
  <data key="d92">3.6</data>
