  <data key="d226">https://addyo.substack.com/p/death-of-the-ide</data>
  <data key="d173">2026-03-20</data>
  <data key="d79">Asynchronous agent orchestration may become the primary developer control surface, while editors remain tools for targeted inspection.</data>
  <data key="d80">Practitioner interpretation; not primary experimental evidence.</data>
  <data key="d81">Teach delegation and inspection as separate activities; Compare orchestration tools on observable state and control; Treat claims about the IDE dying as forecasts.</data>
  <data key="d82">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d86">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d85">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d83">B</data>
  <data key="d87">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d93">Osmani discusses workbench and control-plane interfaces for coordinating cloud and local coding agents. The claim is about a shift in where engineers plan, inspect and approve work, not proof that code editors will disappear.</data>
  <data key="d264">Asynchronous agent orchestration may become the primary developer control surface, while editors remain tools for targeted inspection.</data>
  <data key="d265">Teach delegation and inspection as separate activities; Compare orchestration tools on observable state and control; Treat claims about the IDE dying as forecasts.</data>
