<node id="O017">
  <data key="d73">The 80% Problem in Agentic Coding</data>
  <data key="d74">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d96">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d75">Skills &amp; learning</data>
  <data key="d76">2026</data>
  <data key="d77">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d78">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-80-problem-in-agentic-coding</data>
  <data key="d168">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-80-problem-in-agentic-coding</data>
  <data key="d226">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-80-problem-in-agentic-coding</data>
  <data key="d173">2026-01-28</data>
  <data key="d79">When agents implement most code, developers can accumulate comprehension debt even while shipping more changes.</data>
  <data key="d80">The 80% figure expresses an illustrative workflow shift; it is not a population-level experimental estimate.</data>
  <data key="d81">Require explain-back, behavioral traces and repair exercises; Measure whether an engineer can modify agent-written code later; Treat the 80% phrase as illustration, not a measured universal ratio.</data>
  <data key="d82">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d86">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d85">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d83">A</data>
  <data key="d87">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d93">Extending his earlier "70% problem", Osmani frames agent-heavy development as an inversion between authorship and understanding. Speed moves the bottleneck toward explaining decisions, tracing behavior and maintaining the code after the generation session ends.</data>
  <data key="d264">When agents implement most code, developers can accumulate comprehension debt even while shipping more changes.</data>
  <data key="d265">Require explain-back, behavioral traces and repair exercises; Measure whether an engineer can modify agent-written code later; Treat the 80% phrase as illustration, not a measured universal ratio.</data>
  <data key="d84">2026</data>
  <data key="d92">10.8</data>
