<node id="O035">
  <data key="d73">AI Won't Kill Junior Devs - But Your Hiring Strategy Might</data>
  <data key="d74">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d96">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d75">Jobs &amp; roles</data>
  <data key="d76">2025</data>
  <data key="d77">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d78">https://addyo.substack.com/p/ai-wont-kill-junior-devs-but-your</data>
  <data key="d168">https://addyo.substack.com/p/ai-wont-kill-junior-devs-but-your</data>
  <data key="d226">https://addyo.substack.com/p/ai-wont-kill-junior-devs-but-your</data>
  <data key="d173">2025-05-10</data>
  <data key="d79">Removing junior engineering positions can damage apprenticeship and the future pool of experienced maintainers.</data>
  <data key="d80">A prescriptive career and hiring essay; no original causal analysis of employment trends.</data>
  <data key="d81">Protect supervised learning opportunities for junior developers; Evaluate apprenticeship capacity alongside coding throughput; Compare hiring claims with primary labor-market studies.</data>
  <data key="d82">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d86">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d85">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d83">A</data>
  <data key="d87">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d93">Osmani argues that early-career roles must evolve toward debugging, system comprehension, collaboration and verification as assistants automate routine output. This is a workforce-development argument, not a causal demonstration that AI has or has not changed entry-level hiring.</data>
  <data key="d264">Removing junior engineering positions can damage apprenticeship and the future pool of experienced maintainers.</data>
  <data key="d265">Protect supervised learning opportunities for junior developers; Evaluate apprenticeship capacity alongside coding throughput; Compare hiring claims with primary labor-market studies.</data>
  <data key="d84">2025</data>
  <data key="d92">12.7</data>
  <data key="d266">C12</data>
  <data key="d267">Direct</data>
  <data key="d94">curated practitioner article (not a research study)</data>
  <data key="d268">false</data>
  <data key="d269">W112;W125;W131</data>
