  <data key="d226">https://addyo.substack.com/p/beyond-the-70-maximizing-the-human</data>
  <data key="d173">2025-03-13</data>
  <data key="d79">The difficult last-mile work in AI-assisted development remains architecture, requirements, integration and verification.</data>
  <data key="d80">The 70/30 split is a practitioner metaphor and is not independently measured in this essay.</data>
  <data key="d81">Distinguish generated scaffold from production-ready change; Teach system-level debugging and design; Avoid quoting 70/30 as a measured software engineering constant.</data>
  <data key="d82">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d86">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d85">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d83">A</data>
  <data key="d87">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d93">Expanding his 2024 "70% problem" framing, Osmani discusses the durable value of engineers who can interpret behavior, handle edge cases and maintain systems. The percentages are rhetorical heuristics for relative task difficulty, not tested ratios of project completion.</data>
  <data key="d264">The difficult last-mile work in AI-assisted development remains architecture, requirements, integration and verification.</data>
  <data key="d265">Distinguish generated scaffold from production-ready change; Teach system-level debugging and design; Avoid quoting 70/30 as a measured software engineering constant.</data>
