  <data key="d86">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d85">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d83">B</data>
  <data key="d87">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d93">Osmani explores a world in which individuals and domain specialists build small personalized applications too narrow to justify traditional development contracts. The claim is about enabling software that previously would not be built at all. It is a forward-looking long-tail demand scenario, not a measured labor substitution, return-on-investment or full lifecycle cost study.</data>
  <data key="d264">Lower creation costs may expand demand for one-off and personal applications, an economic hypothesis distinct from measured productivity or employment effects.</data>
  <data key="d265">Separate prototype creation cost from lifecycle cost; Evaluate new demand and substitution scenarios independently; Mark market forecasts as hypotheses.</data>
