  <data key="d79">The final analysis of the publication’s 2026 reader survey examines team adoption, codebase quality, less-experienced engineers and differing experiences of focus and cognitive load.</data>
  <data key="d264">The final analysis of the publication’s 2026 reader survey examines team adoption, codebase quality, less-experienced engineers and differing experiences of focus and cognitive load.</data>
  <data key="d93">The final analysis of the publication’s 2026 reader survey examines team adoption, codebase quality, less-experienced engineers and differing experiences of focus and cognitive load. It argues that existing engineering practices shape perceived benefits.</data>
  <data key="d265">Self-reported benefits coexist with maintenance and learning concerns. This is another analysis of the same survey cohort, not an independent replication of its earlier installments.</data>
  <data key="d304">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d80">Self-selected respondents and subjective reports; shared 2026 survey dataset with AI Tooling 2026 and key trends Part 1. The author reports 906 responses for the shared 2026 cohort. This is not an independently counted sample; comparisons with other survey waves are not a matched longitudinal panel. Subscriber-only article remainder not reviewed. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d81">The same 2026 survey links reported adoption with learning and maintenance concerns, not measured retained skill.</data>
