  <data key="d79">Orosz traces Ralph-style loops, scheduled triggers and goal-oriented harness features.</data>
  <data key="d264">Orosz traces Ralph-style loops, scheduled triggers and goal-oriented harness features.</data>
  <data key="d93">Orosz traces Ralph-style loops, scheduled triggers and goal-oriented harness features. Developer accounts include useful maintenance automation alongside drift, token spending and disappointment with unnecessary orchestration.</data>
  <data key="d265">The article questions whether custom loops remain useful as harnesses absorb them. Context design, bounded scope and measurable benefit are more defensible than assuming that more looping improves results.</data>
  <data key="d304">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d80">P110 directly links https://addyosmani.com/blog/loop-engineering/ (7 June 2026). O012 catalogues the substantially matching Substack edition (8 June 2026). Treat the edge as discussion of the same underlying work via an alternate edition, not an exact-URL citation. The paid remainder and comparative loop outcomes remain unreviewed. Subscriber-only article remainder not reviewed. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d81">The critique challenges unnecessary custom orchestration, not the need for bounded execution, context and independent assurance.</data>
