<node id="OCC:SDD2:C4">
  <data key="d73">Supply-Chain Risk Audit</data>
  <data key="d74">practice_occurrence</data>
  <data key="d357">OCC</data>
  <data key="d358">V03</data>
  <data key="d378">F06</data>
  <data key="d383">Pattern C4</data>
  <data key="d355">AI_SDD_ENG_V2/chapters/chapter15_patterns.tex</data>
  <data key="d384">502</data>
  <data key="d385">535</data>
  <data key="d356">4830546709bc5f2eb4641c47add3bb9992eb9089497514ac25f321b33f5f92aa</data>
  <data key="d386">["\\paragraph{Consequences.} \\emph{Benefits.} Sharp reduction in dependency-introduced CVEs; defence against slopsquatting; the maintenance signal helps teams catch the slow decline of packages they were going to depend on; the skill's findings become part of the architecture decision record. \\emph{Trade-offs.} Requires up-to-date advisory data (the skill's value collapses if its sources lag); the maintenance signal is heuristic and can blacklist healthy single-maintainer projects; review fatigue if the project pulls in many small dependencies at once."]</data>
  <data key="d183">HISTORICAL_SOURCE_OCCURRENCE</data>
  <data key="d80">Attribution retained as written in the supplied source, not independently verified inventorship.</data>
</node>
<node id="OCC:SDD2:C5">
  <data key="d73">Adversarial Agent Pattern</data>
  <data key="d74">practice_occurrence</data>
  <data key="d357">OCC</data>
  <data key="d358">V03</data>
  <data key="d378">F07</data>
  <data key="d383">Pattern C5</data>
  <data key="d355">AI_SDD_ENG_V2/chapters/chapter15_patterns.tex</data>
  <data key="d384">536</data>
  <data key="d385">572</data>
  <data key="d356">4830546709bc5f2eb4641c47add3bb9992eb9089497514ac25f321b33f5f92aa</data>
  <data key="d386">["\\paragraph{Context.} A specification has been approved and an agent (or chain of agents) has begun executing it. The specification is the authoritative source of truth, but the act of execution---turning prose into code, tests, configurations---introduces interpretive freedom. Whoever does the building also has the strongest incentive to declare the work done.", "\\paragraph{Problem.} A single agent that both implements and verifies its own output is structurally biased toward declaring success. Self-verifying agents rationalise away gaps between the artifact and the spec, hallucinate the existence of behaviours they did not implement, and---most diagnostically---``just change the test'' when the code under construction cannot be made to pass. Kent Beck identifies this as the dominant LLM failure mode in test-first workflows: the model treats the test as another mutable artifact rather than as the contract the code must satisfy. Without an external check whose incentive is inverted, errors compound silently across phases.", "  \\node[pcin] (spec) {SPEC\\\\(source of truth)};", "\\paragraph{Consequences.} \\emph{Benefits.} Hallucinated completions are caught at the role boundary rather than after merge. The read-only sandbox makes ``just change the test'' architecturally impossible: the Verifier has no write privilege over the artifact under audit. Failure feedback is structured (spec~$\\to$~Coordinator) rather than ad hoc, which keeps the spec the single source of truth and prevents the Implementor and Verifier from negotiating their way to a passing build. The pattern composes cleanly with gate enforcement (Pattern~C1) because the Verifier's verdict is the natural input to Gate~3 (verification gate). \\emph{Trade-offs.} Inference cost roughly doubles per task because the Verifier re-reads the spec independently. Latency increases because verification cannot be parallelised with implementation without losing the inverted incentive (a Verifier that watches the Implementor work is no longer adversarial). Coordinator design becomes the highest-leverage decision: a weak Coordinator that decomposes badly will produce both bad implementations and irrelevant verifications."]</data>
  <data key="d183">HISTORICAL_SOURCE_OCCURRENCE</data>
  <data key="d80">Attribution retained as written in the supplied source, not independently verified inventorship.</data>
</node>
<node id="OCC:SDD2:D1">
  <data key="d73">One-Item-Per-Cycle</data>
  <data key="d74">practice_occurrence</data>
