<node id="OCC:SDD2:D2">
  <data key="d73">Evidence-Pack Provenance</data>
  <data key="d74">practice_occurrence</data>
  <data key="d357">OCC</data>
  <data key="d358">V03</data>
  <data key="d378">F07</data>
  <data key="d383">Pattern D2</data>
  <data key="d355">AI_SDD_ENG_V2/chapters/chapter15_patterns.tex</data>
  <data key="d384">607</data>
  <data key="d385">642</data>
  <data key="d356">4830546709bc5f2eb4641c47add3bb9992eb9089497514ac25f321b33f5f92aa</data>
  <data key="d386">["%  Sources: Ralph Copilot's per-cycle documentation model.]", "\\paragraph{Solution.} Every autonomous cycle produces a structured \\textbf{evidence pack}\\,---\\,three artifacts at minimum, written to a per-cycle directory under \\code{.specify/evidence/}. The \\code{prompt.md} captures the exact input the agent received: the spec excerpt, the constitution sections, the prior artifacts, the task statement. The \\code{result.md} captures what the agent did: the diff, the test results, the tools invoked, the decisions made and their rationale. The \\code{verdict.md} captures the agent's self-assessment: did the cycle meet the acceptance criteria? Which evidence supports that claim? Which open questions remain? The pack is committed to the repository (it is part of the source of truth, not a transient log) and is linked from \\code{tasks.md} so the trail from item to cycle to evidence is one click."]</data>
  <data key="d183">HISTORICAL_SOURCE_OCCURRENCE</data>
  <data key="d80">Attribution retained as written in the supplied source, not independently verified inventorship.</data>
</node>
<node id="OCC:SDD2:D3">
  <data key="d73">Budget-Gated Autonomy</data>
  <data key="d74">practice_occurrence</data>
  <data key="d357">OCC</data>
  <data key="d358">V03</data>
  <data key="d378">F03</data>
  <data key="d383">Pattern D3</data>
  <data key="d355">AI_SDD_ENG_V2/chapters/chapter15_patterns.tex</data>
  <data key="d384">643</data>
  <data key="d385">676</data>
  <data key="d356">4830546709bc5f2eb4641c47add3bb9992eb9089497514ac25f321b33f5f92aa</data>
  <data key="d386">["%  Sources: Spec Loop Engine's budget model (generalized to inference calls).]", "\\paragraph{Context.} An autonomous run has been launched. The agent has a backlog, a goal, and a model API key with a generous quota. Without an explicit upper bound on resource consumption, the run could plausibly continue for hours or days."]</data>
  <data key="d183">HISTORICAL_SOURCE_OCCURRENCE</data>
  <data key="d80">Attribution retained as written in the supplied source, not independently verified inventorship.</data>
</node>
<node id="OCC:SDD2:D4">
  <data key="d73">Escalation Protocol</data>
  <data key="d74">practice_occurrence</data>
  <data key="d357">OCC</data>
  <data key="d358">V03</data>
  <data key="d378">F11</data>
  <data key="d383">Pattern D4</data>
  <data key="d355">AI_SDD_ENG_V2/chapters/chapter15_patterns.tex</data>
  <data key="d384">677</data>
  <data key="d385">778</data>
  <data key="d356">4830546709bc5f2eb4641c47add3bb9992eb9089497514ac25f321b33f5f92aa</data>
  <data key="d386">["%  Sources: BMAD's explicit handoff protocol; AI-RPI's escalation model.]", "\\paragraph{A1 --- Constitution-Driven Development.} Enterprise SDD generates the constitution through the \\code{@constitution} agent during \\code{sdd init}; the output lives in \\code{.specify/memory/constitution.md} and is loaded by every agent's reading procedure. Update before the first feature, then amend explicitly through a dedicated PR with team sign-off; never edit the constitution as a side effect of a feature PR. Adapted from Spec Kit's constitutional governance and Karpathy's \\code{CLAUDE.md} discipline.", "\\paragraph{A2 --- Clarify Before Plan.} Enterprise SDD wires this into Phase~1.3: \\code{@clarification} runs after \\code{@requirement-analyst} and before Gate~1. The P1/P2/P3 tiering uses the same priority taxonomy as the runtime escalation protocol (Pattern~D4), so the team learns one vocabulary for ``how blocking is this?'' Adapted from Spec Kit's \\code{/clarify} template and the \\code{CLARIFY\\_BEFORE\\_PLAN} rule first articulated in GSD.", "\\paragraph{A3 --- Progressive Specification.} Enterprise SDD's \\code{sdd new --progressive} mode marks the spec with the progressive flag and requires every Gate~1 check to compare the current spec against the previous iteration\\,---\\,if no \\code{[NEEDS-CLARIFICATION]} markers were resolved, the gate fails. Use only on well-understood feature \\emph{types} (UI iterations, integration extensions); set an iteration budget (typically 3) and treat budget exhaustion as the trigger to elevate the feature back to standard pipeline. Adapted from Spec Loop Engine's iterative refinement model.", "\\paragraph{A4 --- Red-Team Specification.} Run \\code{sdd skill run red-team-spec &lt;feature-id&gt;} after the author has self-reviewed and before Gate~1 is requested. The skill's instruction explicitly forbids the agent from confirming what the spec does well; its only output is failure modes. Findings flow into the same gap-closure analysis (Pattern~A5) used for traceability. The pattern is one of three Enterprise~SDD mechanisms that implement the inverted-incentive principle from the Adversarial Agent Pattern (C5). Adapted from security red-teaming practice.", "\\paragraph{A5 --- Gap-Closure Analysis.} Run \\code{sdd analyze --gaps} on brownfield projects after \\code{sdd ingest} has imported the existing repository structure. Use the prioritised orphan list to drive a small allocation of each sprint to retroactive specification (typically 10--20\\,\\%); resist the temptation to declare a ``spec sprint'' that pauses feature delivery, which historically produces specs nobody references. Adapted from AI-RPI Protocol's traceability model.", "\\paragraph{B3 --- Anti-Sycophancy Enforcement.} Enterprise SDD ships the rule as part of the shared \\code{all-agents.instructions.md} so that every agent inherits the same wording; individual agents may extend the rule but cannot weaken it. The rule references the constitution, plan, and architecture pattern files explicitly, so the agent's challenges are anchored in artifacts rather than in vibes. Adapted from Karpathy's anti-sycophancy discipline and GSD's numbered rules.", "\\paragraph{B4 --- Convergence Review.} Enterprise SDD invokes convergence review through \\code{sdd gate check --convergence}, reserved for Gate~2 architectural decisions during autonomous execution. The two-model orchestration is wired into the autonomy policy so that budget accounting (Pattern~D3) accommodates the doubled cost. Adapted from the multi-model debate pattern in advanced LLM research.", "\\paragraph{D1 --- One-Item-Per-Cycle.} The boundary is enforced by \\code{autonomy-policy.instructions.md}: the policy contains a hard rule that no agent may begin a second item within a single cycle, regardless of remaining budget. Adapted from Ralph Copilot's one-item protocol and combined with Spec Loop Engine's per-cycle audit journal.", "\\paragraph{D2 --- Evidence-Pack Provenance.} Evidence packs are required in Autonomous-Governed mode and recommended in Autonomous-Guided mode; the autonomy policy refuses to mark a cycle complete without all three files present and structurally valid. Pair with the Adversarial Agent Pattern (C5): the Verifier's read-only audit produces its own contribution to the evidence pack, separate from the Implementor's, so the reviewer can see both perspectives. Adapted from Ralph Copilot's per-cycle documentation model.", "\\paragraph{D3 --- Budget-Gated Autonomy.} The default budget is set per mode in the autonomy policy; \\code{--autonomy-budget} overrides it on the CLI; \\code{sdd status --autonomy} reports remaining budget at any time during a run. Budget exhaustion produces the same handoff format as escalation (Pattern~D4) so the team has one vocabulary for ``the agent stopped, here is the state.'' Adapted from Spec Loop Engine's budget model and generalised from cycles to inference calls.", "\\paragraph{D4 --- Escalation Protocol.} Enterprise SDD encodes the three tiers in \\code{autonomy-policy.instructions.md}; escalation triggers (blocker, low-confidence, test failures, gate boundary) are listed in the policy with example classifications. Each cycle's evidence pack includes an \\textit{escalation log} with one entry per ambiguity encountered. Adapted from BMAD's explicit handoff protocol and AI-RPI's escalation model.", "\\item Each pattern was adapted from at least one public framework; knowing the origin helps in understanding the context in which the pattern is most effective", "\\item Patterns are not checklists; they are starting points for understanding a recurring problem and adapting the solution to your context"]</data>
  <data key="d183">HISTORICAL_SOURCE_OCCURRENCE</data>
  <data key="d80">Attribution retained as written in the supplied source, not independently verified inventorship.</data>
</node>
<node id="OCC:AGENT:01">
