<node id="OCC:AGENT:17">
  <data key="d73">Blast Radius Containment</data>
  <data key="d74">practice_occurrence</data>
  <data key="d357">OCC</data>
  <data key="d358">V04</data>
  <data key="d378">F06</data>
  <data key="d383">Blast Radius Containment</data>
  <data key="d355">ai-auto_eng/AI_AUTO_ENG/chapters/chapter09_patterns.tex</data>
  <data key="d384">310</data>
  <data key="d385">317</data>
  <data key="d356">3c88f9e5d7b0288e46de69503668ac80ecb7778165d80bf74eab10da30c4e2ba</data>
  <data key="d386">["\\textbf{Solution:} Contain the agent's ``blast radius'' through layered isolation: sandboxed execution environments (Docker, E2B), minimal credential exposure (only necessary API keys), network isolation (egress restrictions), and resource limits (CPU, memory, disk).", "\\textbf{Ralph connection:} This is the sandboxing strategy from \\chapref{ch:safety} formalized as a pattern. The pattern catalogue adds a structured approach: enumerate all resources the agent can access, then systematically restrict each to the minimum required. Huntley's philosophy—``It's not if it gets popped, it's when. And what is the blast radius?''—is the pattern's guiding principle."]</data>
  <data key="d183">HISTORICAL_SOURCE_OCCURRENCE</data>
  <data key="d80">Attribution retained as written in the supplied source, not independently verified inventorship.</data>
</node>
<node id="OCC:AGENT:18">
  <data key="d73">PII and Secret Scrubbing</data>
  <data key="d74">practice_occurrence</data>
  <data key="d357">OCC</data>
