<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0001">
  <data key="d73">Research on developers' activity makes the distinction tangible. In their field study of professional developers, Xia and colleagues monitored work across seven</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">19</data>
  <data key="d388">19</data>
  <data key="d389">Research on developers' activity makes the distinction tangible. In their field study of professional developers, Xia and colleagues monitored work across seven projects, 79 people, and 3,244 hours in the \emph{author-hosted manuscript}. The authors classified an average of 57.62\% of the observed time as program-comprehension activity. Their measurement deliberately extended beyond an IDE: the activities included searching or reading material in browsers and working with documentation. Table 7 of the accessible author manuscript breaks down where comprehension occurred, with average shares of 19.95\% for IDEs, 27.26\% for web browsers, and 10.38\% for document editors across the observed projects. These are categories derived from their observation and inference method, not a prescription for how engineers should schedule their days. \autocite{W170}</data>
  <data key="d259">5b7d85b92bc133ef848b2dbe011f8748bf68511ed0c6e889fb1a14806c1bc143</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W170"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0002">
  <data key="d73">A smaller earlier study by Minelli, Mocci and Lanza examined fine-grained activity in the Pharo development environment. It recorded approximately 740 sessions </data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">21</data>
  <data key="d388">21</data>
  <data key="d389">A smaller earlier study by Minelli, Mocci and Lanza examined fine-grained activity in the Pharo development environment. It recorded approximately 740 sessions from 18 participants, totaling around 200 hours. Its Figure 3 assigns roughly 70\% of the instrumented activity to understanding, compared with about 5\% to editing. That result is sometimes repeated as if it describes every programming job. It does not. The sample mixed students and professionals, the IDE was specialized, and the authors inferred categories from interface events under explicit assumptions. The later, broader field study found a different percentage using a different observation frame. Both studies are useful precisely because they show that reading and reconstructing system behavior consume substantial attention while resisting a universal, context-free ratio. \autocite{W176}</data>
  <data key="d259">a64271b9efca88870fe3c1cfe992bd4871fb86d0a803d879c3e3671e7a937e25</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W176"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0003">
  <data key="d73">As the scope expands, the engineer's role changes from selecting one suggestion at a time to supervising a sequence of decisions that may already have affected </data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">35</data>
  <data key="d388">35</data>
  <data key="d389">As the scope expands, the engineer's role changes from selecting one suggestion at a time to supervising a sequence of decisions that may already have affected the workspace. The distinction has empirical grounding, although the specific effects remain context-dependent. Chen and colleagues compared GitHub Copilot with OpenHands in a controlled user study involving 20 recruited students, many of whom reported some professional programming experience. Participants worked on both systems, with 40 minutes allocated to each condition, using tasks such as fixing repository issues, adding features and conducting research. The study design deliberately captured tool interactions and final code snapshots as different measurements. This provides a useful illustration of different human--tool workflows, but its small and selected population is not proof that one delegation level will always produce better enterprise software. \autocite{W002}</data>
  <data key="d259">8b8db35a0de8ec233d323fb3a31efc100d01e606247c1c18f8ff7fde06740f39</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W002"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0004">
  <data key="d73">A very different kind of evidence comes from Liu and colleagues' characterization of GitHub Copilot production-scale traces. Their June 2026 sampled dataset cov</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">37</data>
  <data key="d388">37</data>
  <data key="d389">A very different kind of evidence comes from Liu and colleagues' characterization of GitHub Copilot production-scale traces. Their June 2026 sampled dataset covered approximately 3.2 million users, 13 million agent sessions and 761 million model calls. At that scale, a user's instruction often begins a sequence of model inferences and tool executions. The paper's first table describes a workload whose machine-side steps can substantially outnumber the user's turns. These are observations about execution structure and inference infrastructure, not about how many resulting patches passed an independent engineering review. \autocite{W012}</data>
  <data key="d259">a39f52b0c6dec052ac3b4b7be3b3112cdcaf04af816f116347f27e1d1f3fca55</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W012"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0005">
  <data key="d73">Jeremy Osborn's essay in \emph{Communications of the ACM} offers a useful framing rather than an experimental estimate. In the portion independently available f</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">55</data>
  <data key="d388">55</data>
  <data key="d389">Jeremy Osborn's essay in \emph{Communications of the ACM} offers a useful framing rather than an experimental estimate. In the portion independently available from ACM's official publication channel, he describes programming as increasingly dependent on reasoning and judgment when assistance reduces the burden of low-level recall. The argument has intuitive force: a developer who no longer writes a serializer manually still needs to decide whether its error handling, data contracts and security assumptions fit the system. It is an \textbf{opinion and synthesis}, however, not a measurement establishing how much time has moved from coding to review. The official text inspected for this chapter is unpaginated and partial; the exact journal-page reference remains an editorial action before publication. \autocite{W122}</data>
  <data key="d259">9fcb10a17890621d5c45e3e2e13a27bcb4d34634bcb3b58c344a1223ee0fecbf</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W122"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0006">
  <data key="d73">Hoda's proposal for \emph{agentic software engineering beyond code} approaches the same development from a broader angle. Its whole-of-process diagram places hu</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">57</data>
  <data key="d388">57</data>
  <data key="d389">Hoda's proposal for \emph{agentic software engineering beyond code} approaches the same development from a broader angle. Its whole-of-process diagram places human and agentic work across requirements, design, development, operations and ethical concerns. This is an explicit research vision, with preliminary principles and vocabulary, rather than evidence that an already deployed autonomous process performs the entire lifecycle reliably. The distinction matters. A vision can organize the questions engineering teams should ask without answering those questions empirically. \autocite{W153}</data>
  <data key="d259">4b52e08521000792bfc44b50573495157cdf2df4648502aaecc0aa00d4c57f8c</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W153"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0007">
  <data key="d73">The research gives reasons to consider this structure, without establishing that these exact gates have been experimentally validated as a package. Farrag's 202</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">73</data>
  <data key="d388">73</data>
  <data key="d389">The research gives reasons to consider this structure, without establishing that these exact gates have been experimentally validated as a package. Farrag's 2026 multivocal review combines 67 selected sources with a separately described \emph{illustrative} four-month exercise across three teams and 14 engineers (eight mid-level, six senior). The paper does not randomize teams or identify an external contemporaneous control group; its lead-time, hotfix and rollback ranges therefore cannot establish the causal efficacy of specification governance. Its proposed separation of specifications and checking remains a design input, not a calibrated performance claim. \autocite{W011}</data>
  <data key="d259">7c329d5e134ddac1bf646b5856250afc830b66f56d5b990d98f25db7e2023ca2</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W011"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0008">
  <data key="d73">Return to the request for configurable approval rules. An agent might have permission to create configuration fixtures, inspect code paths, run tests and prepar</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">88</data>
  <data key="d388">88</data>
  <data key="d389">Return to the request for configurable approval rules. An agent might have permission to create configuration fixtures, inspect code paths, run tests and prepare a pull request. That permission does not establish the legal meaning of approval, the acceptable residual risk of a routing failure, or who is entitled to waive a failed check. Chen and colleagues' controlled comparison makes the interaction difference concrete, because the two tools induced different patterns of human participation. It did not test whether a coding agent should be allowed to accept financial policy changes. Hoda's whole-of-process vision similarly places agency on a continuum within human-controlled socio-technical work; it offers a conceptual map rather than a production authorization matrix. \autocite{W153,W002}</data>
  <data key="d259">0666ccc0119cd6c581c59779f303cf49c4a9565524cd588f440687b2296a7568</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W002", "W153"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0009">
  <data key="d73">LaToza, Venolia and DeLine examined developers' work habits through two surveys and eleven interviews. They found that engineers often expended significant effo</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">100</data>
  <data key="d388">100</data>
  <data key="d389">LaToza, Venolia and DeLine examined developers' work habits through two surveys and eleven interviews. They found that engineers often expended significant effort recovering implicit knowledge by exploring code or interrupting colleagues, while design documents failed to capture enough rationale for easy recovery. This was research from 2006, before current coding agents, yet the underlying constraint remains recognizable. Systems do not store all their meaningful requirements in a single file; much of the explanation for their shape is distributed among people, operational history and partial artifacts. \autocite{W186}</data>
  <data key="d259">571280447c9072ac2b6b31e70b0c9e24f6f531e43b1e9cb27b811e0be7fded69</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W186"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0010">
  <data key="d73">Xia and colleagues add a useful observational perspective: comprehension occurs across several tools, not only through source browsing. Web searches, documentat</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">102</data>
  <data key="d388">102</data>
  <data key="d389">Xia and colleagues add a useful observational perspective: comprehension occurs across several tools, not only through source browsing. Web searches, documentation and communication are part of the activity being measured. An AI agent that reads the repository but cannot access, authenticate or interpret the relevant external context may build a coherent model of the code while missing the intended behavior. Conversely, giving it unrestricted access to external systems may expose secrets or permit actions beyond the task. Better context must be combined with bounded authority. \autocite{W170}</data>
  <data key="d259">6a42b43f719c7bdea50d4f787d53b1b1cefc4ba27b78f4c4f72b04eb8c11b40a</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W170"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0011">
  <data key="d73">Cui and colleagues studied workplace rollout of a coding assistant and reported greater task output, operationalized in the inspected working manuscript through</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">120</data>
  <data key="d388">120</data>
  <data key="d389">Cui and colleagues studied workplace rollout of a coding assistant and reported greater task output, operationalized in the inspected working manuscript through measures including pull requests. That endpoint is useful for studying the intervention, but does not directly measure delivered production capability. \autocite{W003}</data>
  <data key="d259">1012060395bc0bb870185a74f5496704fad07b61161bad51504520e44899fa5c</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W003"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0012">
  <data key="d73">Becker and colleagues instead timed experienced maintainers working on issues in repositories they knew well. Their early-2025 randomized comparison reported lo</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">122</data>
  <data key="d388">122</data>
  <data key="d389">Becker and colleagues instead timed experienced maintainers working on issues in repositories they knew well. Their early-2025 randomized comparison reported longer completion time when AI was allowed. The difference from the workplace study begins with the work and measurement settings, not necessarily with a defective experiment. \autocite{W023}</data>
  <data key="d259">f29f0dd009cba5ad176293bc48a9777cb872b211232335ab75a72a1b02544f87</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W023"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0013">
  <data key="d73">Ownership research explains another part of the example's design. Greiler, Herzig and Czerwonka replicated work linking source ownership patterns to bug-pronene</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">138</data>
  <data key="d388">138</data>
  <data key="d389">Ownership research explains another part of the example's design. Greiler, Herzig and Czerwonka replicated work linking source ownership patterns to bug-proneness, examining four Microsoft products. They found meaningful correlations between ownership measures and bug labels. That supports asking who knows and maintains the routing code; it does not prove that assigning a new owner or increasing ownership concentration would causally fix our fabricated defect. Likewise, Xia's comprehension study supports the need to inspect existing behavior, not a particular policy for financial transactions. The service and all its outputs are teaching artifacts, not observations made by those researchers. \autocite{W175,W170}</data>
  <data key="d259">4abc2c16fe4298494d7ac8f5d41911f87fa478b38759dd8fe3cbcba5e361f89c</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W170", "W175"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0014">
  <data key="d73">Demirer, Musolff and Yang examine tool adoption at several production endpoints and report that increases in coding activity attenuate at later stages. Their ve</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">150</data>
  <data key="d388">150</data>
  <data key="d389">Demirer, Musolff and Yang examine tool adoption at several production endpoints and report that increases in coding activity attenuate at later stages. Their versioned observational analysis reinforces the need to distinguish commits, projects and releases; it does not establish a conversion factor between them. Chapter~\ref{chap:02} examines the estimates, while Appendix~\ref{app:methodology} records version and verification limits. \autocite{W010}</data>
  <data key="d259">9956edbb0544b8a24331da7d30f6cbcb9372949e2155718220b0192183bedaf4</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W010"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0015">
  <data key="d73">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/01_Figure_1_1_Architect.jpg}
\caption{From coding tools to whole-process so</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
