</data>
  <data key="d259">8c8343faf5596c94275f8fd449eaaf2746f184541c5647ad1f5a6ef52298159c</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W153"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0016">
  <data key="d73">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/02_Figure_1_2_Architect.jpg}
\caption{The software lifecycle extends beyond</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">figures/source_redraws/figure_ch01_02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">29</data>
  <data key="d388">33</data>
  <data key="d389">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/02_Figure_1_2_Architect.jpg}
\caption{The software lifecycle extends beyond generated code. Conceptual redraw of W153, original Figure 2; \workref{W153}. The full lifecycle is a research vision, not a validated universal process.}
\label{fig:ch01-research-2}
\end{figure}
</data>
  <data key="d259">b53dad79bc5b082200ecc1b264f138a6982b17b0c03f1abd32b6c4a61fb17d89</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W153"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0017">
  <data key="d73">Ziegler and colleagues\textquotesingle{} work on neural code completion helps frame the local interaction: suggestion acceptance and perceived usefulness matter</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">19</data>
  <data key="d388">19</data>
  <data key="d389">Ziegler and colleagues\textquotesingle{} work on neural code completion helps frame the local interaction: suggestion acceptance and perceived usefulness matter to the person at the keyboard, but are not interchangeable with system throughput. Demirer, Musolff and Yang explicitly separate commits, projects and releases. Their September 2026 NBER revision reports cumulative increases in commits of approximately 30\%, 180\% and 240\% across successive tool generations, while the last effect falls to about 80\% at the project level and 30\% at the release level. These are observational estimates for their sampled developers and tool periods, not conversion factors that can be imposed on an arbitrary team. \autocite{W009,W010}</data>
  <data key="d259">2af4106223ea77adfc62f387123d7c66ed14b8fd1c884b2385df23e3f0142217</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W009", "W010"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0018">
  <data key="d73">Cui and colleagues report three field experiments involving 4,867 developers and a pooled increase of 26.08\% in completed tasks, with a reported standard error</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">35</data>
  <data key="d388">35</data>
  <data key="d389">Cui and colleagues report three field experiments involving 4,867 developers and a pooled increase of 26.08\% in completed tasks, with a reported standard error of 10.3 percentage points, in the published Management Science abstract. The journal\textquotesingle s detailed tables still require parity checking against earlier manuscript versions, so we use the publisher-confirmed aggregate without inventing a table citation. \autocite{W003}</data>
  <data key="d259">2a1a410ec5fa2f2c40fde7a3ea0be3dd7eabcd13a183fc5552843a92cc8325ab</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W003"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0019">
  <data key="d73">Paradis and colleagues randomized 96 Google engineers and estimated roughly 21\% less time spent on a particular complex task with AI assistance; the authors em</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">37</data>
  <data key="d388">37</data>
  <data key="d389">Paradis and colleagues randomized 96 Google engineers and estimated roughly 21\% less time spent on a particular complex task with AI assistance; the authors emphasize that their interval is wide and that the result should not be generalized across tools and workflows. \autocite{W008}</data>
  <data key="d259">3290869fe474e407505ab506e8d41c18bc7aab4daa13fde86c3eed4d4fec4ee4</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W008"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0020">
  <data key="d73">Two experiments can disagree without one being defective. Becker and colleagues studied sixteen experienced open-source developers completing 246 tasks in repos</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">47</data>
  <data key="d388">47</data>
  <data key="d389">Two experiments can disagree without one being defective. Becker and colleagues studied sixteen experienced open-source developers completing 246 tasks in repositories they knew well. In their early-2025 tool setting, allowing AI increased completion time by about 19\%. Developers had expected acceleration, and after participating still believed the tool had helped. Paradis and colleagues\textquotesingle{} Google trial instead estimated a roughly 21\% reduction in task time in its own environment. \autocite{W023,W008} Figure~\ref{fig:ch02-research-1} isolates the first study's disagreement: both subjective estimates fall below zero, meaning less task time, while the measured effect is above zero. Retrospective confidence therefore remains a different signal from timed performance.</data>
  <data key="d259">5e12e33917104b3e58a65a1d58a31f5d8f055e3862d1017b55695fdbcc29aa8e</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W008", "W023"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0021">
  <data key="d73">Agarwal and colleagues examine code and maintenance signals associated with AI development tools; He and colleagues analyze the trade-off between short-term vel</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">65</data>
  <data key="d388">65</data>
  <data key="d389">Agarwal and colleagues examine code and maintenance signals associated with AI development tools; He and colleagues analyze the trade-off between short-term velocity and longer-term quality in Cursor-associated development. These studies motivate a joint productivity-and-maintainability lens, but their particular measurements and causal identification should be verified against the exact source versions before their detailed coefficients are used. \autocite{W001,W004}</data>
  <data key="d259">c44ade4809d0f4d27bd3079e225eb5f18c784932dc5166a88461d3c863719245</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W001", "W004"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0022">
  <data key="d73">Software work moves through a chain of constraints. A developer can produce more patches than reviewers can evaluate, and a reviewer can approve more changes th</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">79</data>
  <data key="d388">79</data>
  <data key="d389">Software work moves through a chain of constraints. A developer can produce more patches than reviewers can evaluate, and a reviewer can approve more changes than a release train can safely deploy. Queueing, dependency coordination and operational validation determine whether local speed becomes end-to-end delivery. The September NBER findings offer an unusually clear illustration: large changes in commits are accompanied by much smaller changes in releases. The authors interpret the attenuation through complementarity and production bottlenecks, but their fitted economic model is not a universal law of software organizations. \autocite{W010}</data>
  <data key="d259">502900d3c2503a52bd4c3e150e2121895dcc64edc60c736a36b5ad034f448b98</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W010"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0023">
  <data key="d73">\noindent\textbf{The apparently finished patch.} Osmani calls attention to a ``70\% problem'' in AI-assisted development: a convincing prototype arrives quickly</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">87</data>
  <data key="d388">87</data>
  <data key="d389">\noindent\textbf{The apparently finished patch.} Osmani calls attention to a ``70\% problem'' in AI-assisted development: a convincing prototype arrives quickly, while the difficult work of integrating it, handling exceptions and sustaining it in production remains \osmanicite{O049}. The percentage belongs to his practitioner shorthand; it is neither a universal completion rate nor a measured treatment effect. His separate review of productivity evidence also distinguishes personal speed from team delivery \osmanicite{O029}. The useful engineering question is therefore not how close the generated patch \emph{looks} to completion, but where its next unresolved gate sits.</data>
  <data key="d259">a35f3c053d328f117e6ff4fa60a75fa34eacc0c9af06470d4307e6757ccdff45</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["O029", "O049"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0024">
  <data key="d73">Studies of Cursor-associated development and autonomous-agent maintenance raise this concern, but static-analysis warnings, cognitive complexity, defects, vulne</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">100</data>
  <data key="d388">100</data>
  <data key="d389">Studies of Cursor-associated development and autonomous-agent maintenance raise this concern, but static-analysis warnings, cognitive complexity, defects, vulnerabilities and incidents are different outcomes. A warning count cannot be described as an observed exploit, and a complexity metric is not itself a measured future outage. We should keep these measures distinct and avoid combining them into a fabricated \textquotesingle quality score.\textquotesingle{} \autocite{W004,W054}</data>
  <data key="d259">9a062329193f916e66784e441f2404904401089603a38af047c5bb66f8384dcb</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W004", "W054"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0025">
  <data key="d73">The early-2025 METR experiment is a useful warning because developers\textquotesingle{} forecasts and retrospective impressions diverged from the measured task-</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">122</data>
  <data key="d388">122</data>
  <data key="d389">The early-2025 METR experiment is a useful warning because developers\textquotesingle{} forecasts and retrospective impressions diverged from the measured task-time effect. The May 2026 METR survey \autocite{W020} retained 349 responses after excluding 10 of 359 unique entries: participants reported median value multipliers across alternative questions of about 1.4--2 times, compared with approximately three times for perceived task speed. Email response rates were around two per cent and roughly 70\% of respondents received compensation, with convenience recruitment. The headline values are therefore \emph{self-reported counterfactual beliefs}, rather than tested throughput multipliers or evidence that 349 projects shipped more value. Later observations can inform hypotheses about tool familiarity, but retrospective perception is not observed elapsed time and neither can be silently treated as a randomized productivity effect. \autocite{W023,W020}</data>
  <data key="d259">d4dbaa491af1bba195ece994bc830509fae4a506f5a9c88cb60760552db635c8</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W020", "W023"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0026">
  <data key="d73">Pre-register task eligibility, assignment, tool versions, success criteria, exclusions, independent review and stopping conditions. Keep every assigned task, in</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter02.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">143</data>
  <data key="d388">143</data>
  <data key="d389">Pre-register task eligibility, assignment, tool versions, success criteria, exclusions, independent review and stopping conditions. Keep every assigned task, including failures and abandonment. Orosz and Laura Tacho's account of company measurement practices combines delivery, quality and developer experience rather than relying on activity alone \oroszcite{P061}. For this pilot, turn that motivation into a measurement contract: each signal needs a unit, collection window and declared decision.</data>
  <data key="d259">19e5e5d7f7e450541808b07e650e10c10cff5eab634ad906b9938d5844cdea7e</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["P061"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0027">
  <data key="d73">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/04_Figure_2_1_Architect.jpg}
\caption{Perceived speed and measured task tim</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">figures/source_redraws/figure_ch02_01.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">34</data>
  <data key="d388">38</data>
