<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0084">
  <data key="d73">Zhao, Zhou and Cohen \autocite{W077} study precisely this misguidance effect in generated unit tests. Their ISSTA 2026 paper uses paired buggy and fixed version</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">17</data>
  <data key="d388">17</data>
  <data key="d389">Zhao, Zhou and Cohen \autocite{W077} study precisely this misguidance effect in generated unit tests. Their ISSTA 2026 paper uses paired buggy and fixed versions from Defects4J; the curated sample contains 318 focal methods covering 233 defects in 17 projects (PDF pp.~3--5, §2.2). Their definition is careful: a test that passes on buggy code is not automatically misguided, because many inputs behave identically in buggy and fixed versions. A genuinely misguided test asserts behavior that fails on the corrected reference (pp.~2--3). Their specification-based prompting reduces the problem in their evaluated setting, although a generated docstring is still an inferred specification, not a stakeholder authorization.</data>
  <data key="d259">bcfb6b8fa8a71102d296c79ae9d5cf733da4e113e9a60081cc319559951efe93</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W077"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0085">
  <data key="d73">Kaufman and colleagues \autocite{W097} add a human-review warning. Their experiment involved 86 Python programmers who judged generated assertions. Accuracy was</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">19</data>
  <data key="d388">19</data>
  <data key="d389">Kaufman and colleagues \autocite{W097} add a human-review warning. Their experiment involved 86 Python programmers who judged generated assertions. Accuracy was 74\% on correct assertions but 49\% on incorrect assertions, despite similar confidence (PDF p.~1, abstract). This does not mean all human reviewers perform at those rates. It means that human inspection, too, benefits from an independent behavioral reference. The book's proposed rule is to label each oracle with its authority and to keep maker-written tests supplementary to independently derived acceptance checks.</data>
  <data key="d259">864d2df3dce82067aceb6a69dc0d30accc4b72e9fa80bbaedbb5fd40a379f726</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W097"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0086">
  <data key="d73">Whitfill and colleagues' METR study \autocite{W111} asks maintainers to judge SWE-bench-passing patches and finds that benchmark success does not guarantee merg</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">31</data>
  <data key="d388">31</data>
  <data key="d389">Whitfill and colleagues' METR study \autocite{W111} asks maintainers to judge SWE-bench-passing patches and finds that benchmark success does not guarantee merge readiness. Its primary source is an online research note, so the audit cites named sections rather than fabricated page numbers. \autocite{W095} discusses lifecycle assurance as a proposed framework; \autocite{W080} concerns unit testing under LLM assistance. Their detailed methods remain to be inspected before numerical findings can be used.</data>
  <data key="d259">69facb3e5e54fe05a9f403756f9fa97bed840a96e6dfd654eafc5fdc02493bd5</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W080", "W095", "W111"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0087">
  <data key="d73">\autocite{W077} compares test generation directly from code with a two-stage procedure that first derives a behavioral docstring and then generates tests from t</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">39</data>
  <data key="d388">39</data>
  <data key="d389">\autocite{W077} compares test generation directly from code with a two-stage procedure that first derives a behavioral docstring and then generates tests from the docstring rather than the buggy body. The paper reports fewer misguided tests and more bug-finding tests under its experimental conditions (PDF pp.~2--4, 6--7). The authors' inferred docstring is not identical to our canonical intention contract: it can itself contain a mistaken assumption. In intent-first verification, the approved intention remains the authority, while inferred specifications are proposals requiring review.</data>
  <data key="d259">28939081001a6e12a5fa32106f8b43a315fe5a719081b28e39dd3273b4379e21</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W077"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0088">
  <data key="d73">\workposs{W101} work on self-review in code modernization motivates further caution, but its detailed numerical findings are not verified in this release. The p</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">43</data>
  <data key="d388">43</data>
  <data key="d389">\workposs{W101} work on self-review in code modernization motivates further caution, but its detailed numerical findings are not verified in this release. The practical distinction is that the maker's self-tests and explanation belong in the evidence bundle while independent tests retain their own provenance and cannot be silently rewritten by the maker.</data>
  <data key="d259">7ce40de662445e9196d3b5cd6014f834f6900f243de5cab70a98229b6a46e434</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W101"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0089">
  <data key="d73">\workposs{W077} buggy/fixed comparison demonstrates the value of holding intended behavior constant across program versions. \workposs{W080} unit-testing resear</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">59</data>
  <data key="d388">59</data>
  <data key="d389">\workposs{W077} buggy/fixed comparison demonstrates the value of holding intended behavior constant across program versions. \workposs{W080} unit-testing research and \workposs{W148} process-model benchmark work provide contrasting research directions, but neither is used here as proof that this exact brownfield workflow is optimal. The proposed procedure is iterative: late intention is versioned and traced rather than becoming an unrecorded prompt or triggering complete regeneration.</data>
  <data key="d259">d2a3e17a339088350dd21e598cdf1fe11c5dca7b34f328712d32dd3320ced3e9</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W077", "W080", "W148"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0090">
  <data key="d73">Finding a counterexample and reproducing its execution are different achievements. Orosz and Elin Nilsson's Antithesis report describes deterministic simulation</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">74</data>
  <data key="d388">74</data>
  <data key="d389">Finding a counterexample and reproducing its execution are different achievements. Orosz and Elin Nilsson's Antithesis report describes deterministic simulation as a way to revisit the conditions behind a failure \oroszcite{P006}. The transferable lesson here is modest: retain enough state and ordering information to challenge the same behavior again.</data>
  <data key="d259">98286006f1cfb37a1b38fd7e6494a223558a085cb9bcc46a8cb8c2cbc645a99e</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["P006"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0091">
  <data key="d73">\autocite{W045} and \autocite{W038} address vulnerabilities and secure code generation, which motivates negative cases but does not prove the completeness of ou</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">81</data>
  <data key="d388">81</data>
  <data key="d389">\autocite{W045} and \autocite{W038} address vulnerabilities and secure code generation, which motivates negative cases but does not prove the completeness of our synthetic security checks. Detailed threat analysis belongs to Chapter 6. Here the engineering objective is to demonstrate that the independent oracle rejects representative wrong implementations, not merely that the intended implementation passes.</data>
  <data key="d259">b4b55430fcf5deaa3f4c38713430cbeb530067223a4308d9753e46e7c9b701f2</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W038", "W045"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0092">
  <data key="d73">\autocite{W111} examines this gap directly by asking maintainers to evaluate SWE-bench-passing patches. The authors' METR report is a primary institutional rese</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">87</data>
  <data key="d388">87</data>
  <data key="d389">\autocite{W111} examines this gap directly by asking maintainers to evaluate SWE-bench-passing patches. The authors' METR report is a primary institutional research note, not a paginated journal paper, and its review protocol should not be generalized to all repositories without qualification. \workposs{W192} AIDev data capture naturally occurring agent-authored pull requests; the W068 and W071 follow-on analyses reuse that shared corpus \autocite{W068,W071}. Their cohorts must not be counted as independently sampled replications. \workposs{W193} MirrorCode exercise instead concerns long-horizon capability. These sources measure different endpoints and cannot be pooled into one acceptance rate.</data>
  <data key="d259">c7eb1b6d902db1bb1ccf14e5b92d4ef2ef3019a05f01c651f7721f9f71de89ea</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W068", "W071", "W111", "W192", "W193"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0093">
  <data key="d73">Independent verification can involve deterministic checks, humans, another language model or a combination. Independence does not follow automatically from usin</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">95</data>
  <data key="d388">95</data>
  <data key="d389">Independent verification can involve deterministic checks, humans, another language model or a combination. Independence does not follow automatically from using a second model name. Two models can share the same faulty requirement, training bias or missing repository context. A human can also be misled by fluent but incorrect assertions, as \autocite{W097} illustrates.</data>
  <data key="d259">3b42315f498991c4d217e383b84641f1398d0936309ccae4d4a9dc91b95061cc</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W097"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0094">
  <data key="d73">Figure~\ref{fig:ch07-research-1} makes the conditioning in \autocite{W079} important: the bars compare how often two models choose the same wrong answer \emph{g</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">97</data>
  <data key="d388">97</data>
  <data key="d389">Figure~\ref{fig:ch07-research-1} makes the conditioning in \autocite{W079} important: the bars compare how often two models choose the same wrong answer \emph{given that both are wrong}. They do not show how often the models fail overall. Agreement above the uniform wrong-choice baseline warns against treating consensus as independent evidence. Together with evaluator self-preference in broader LLM judgments \autocite{W082}, it motivates diversifying evidence sources; its quantitative transfer to software defect detection remains unverified.</data>
  <data key="d259">12b8c6b6ca538821919bfd1c9f3a6b882e99403d83476af691041b0fd3d5897f</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W079", "W082"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0095">
  <data key="d73">Every attempt retains a task identifier, code hash, tool version, diff, tests, failure reason, time or cost budget and resulting state. \autocite{W101}, \autoci</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">131</data>
  <data key="d388">131</data>
  <data key="d389">Every attempt retains a task identifier, code hash, tool version, diff, tests, failure reason, time or cost budget and resulting state. \autocite{W101}, \autocite{W095} and \autocite{W151} motivate caution about self-review and policy separation, but do not validate this exact retry policy. The steps in Figure~\ref{fig:ch07-research-2} represent distinct obligations: establish scope and contract, test behavior, review the evidence, and decide on release. Passing the test-oracle step does not satisfy the neighboring obligations. The staircase is a checklist of evidence boundaries, not a calibrated rise in reliability.</data>
  <data key="d259">640454a5b25b369e6987867b3193dc4615ca7c931fc1aef66bd43af80253cdab</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W095", "W101", "W151"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0096">
  <data key="d73">\autocite{W111} explains why benchmark success is insufficient for merge readiness. \workposs{W099} intent/drift research and \workposs{W150} study of repositor</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
  <data key="d355">chapters/chapter07.tex</data>
  <data key="d387">154</data>
  <data key="d388">154</data>
  <data key="d389">\autocite{W111} explains why benchmark success is insufficient for merge readiness. \workposs{W099} intent/drift research and \workposs{W150} study of repository AI policies provide context for review attention and organizational rules, but their full manuscripts still require final audit. The dossier format itself is the book's engineering proposal.</data>
  <data key="d259">b1df2c0f6dc5104d591274f1c5705edafbfadd26e6fad80a769c152daa784801</data>
  <data key="d183">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
  <data key="d390">["W099", "W111", "W150"]</data>
  <data key="d391">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
</node>
<node id="V10:TEXTREF:0097">
  <data key="d73">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/17_Figure_7_1_Architect.jpg}
\caption{Independent labels matter when checke</data>
  <data key="d74">cited_passage</data>
  <data key="d357">CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d358">V06</data>
